WEBVTT

00:16:29.042 --> 00:16:30.309
So if you want somebody

00:16:30.333 --> 00:16:32.684
to have positive expectations
about your behavior,

00:16:32.708 --> 00:16:35.601
if you want them to be comfortable
being vulnerable with you,

00:16:35.625 --> 00:16:37.684
then you shouldn't
hide anything from them.

00:16:37.708 --> 00:16:39.518
And then Rachel said, "Wait a minute,

00:16:39.542 --> 00:16:42.268
If you trust me, you don't need to
know what I'm hiding."

00:16:42.292 --> 00:16:45.434
AG: And oh, that is a big frameshift.

00:16:45.458 --> 00:16:47.684
EP: So I looked at it developmentally.

00:16:47.708 --> 00:16:50.934
I tried to apply it,
and I thought of it actually,

00:16:50.958 --> 00:16:52.976
where does this concept come from?

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.601
And I don't know where she rooted it,

00:16:55.625 --> 00:16:58.268
but for me, it was --
you know, around eight months,

00:16:58.292 --> 00:17:01.268
the kid takes a little object
and they drop it.